+# Field contracts
+
+Verify field-specific behavior against current source and the applicable design.
+
+Use these standards for configurable form fields unless a field-specific design explicitly documents an exception.
+
+### Configuration-editor ordering
+
+Order the settings in each field editor as follows:
+
+1. Label
+2. Placeholder, when the field supports one
+3. Default Value, when the field supports one
+4. Shared validation settings, including validation type and custom error message
+5. Field-specific settings
+6. Disabled
+
+The shared validation row is always represented by `ValidationConfigEditorComponent`. Its
+`Validation` dropdown must contain the field’s supported validation choices, including
+`Required` when applicable; do not add a separate Required checkbox or toggle beside the
+field controls. A domain-specific label such as `Default Group` still occupies the
+Default Value position in this order. `Disabled` is a separate final checkbox after all
+field-specific settings and must write `renderer.config.disabled`.
+
+The shared configuration modal owns the Override ID and ID controls. They appear after the field-specific editor and should not be duplicated inside individual field editors. Omit settings that are not applicable; do not add a placeholder merely to satisfy the ordering.
+
+### Placeholder versus default value
+
+A placeholder is display-only guidance shown while a field is empty. It is not a default value and must not:
+
+- populate `formData`;
+- become the Angular control value;
+- suppress a configured `defaultValue`; or
+- be submitted as entered data.
+
+Only expose a placeholder editor input when the field’s runtime control supports it. Keep placeholder text separate from option/default-value logic.
+
+### Default-value behavior
+
+Use this contract for fields that support configured defaults. Keep field-specific normalization and validation in the shared default-value utility rather than implementing separate precedence logic in each editor, adapter, or renderer.
+
+- Store the configured default in `renderer.config.defaultValue`. A configured default is authoring configuration, not a duplicate `formData` entry.
+- The builder is WYSIWYG: show the configured default when the field has no valid current value, including while the field is disabled in the editor. Any value entered through builder interaction synchronizes to `renderer.config.defaultValue`; it must not be emitted as ordinary builder `formData`. Clearing the value removes the configured default and the corresponding builder data entry.
+- Initial runtime hydration resolves a valid current value, then a valid configured default, then empty. After hydration, an explicit runtime clear is a current value rather than a missing value and must remain clear in the runtime snapshot.
+- Normalize values at the shared boundary according to the field’s value type and validity rules. Preserve configured option value types.
+- When loading a persisted builder draft, use the shared normalizer to promote legacy values into `renderer.config.defaultValue`, with an existing valid configured default taking precedence, only when compatibility or draft migration is part of the field-specific design. Otherwise, do not add migration solely to preserve old definitions. Remove only migrated field IDs from builder `formData`; preserve unrelated data.
+- Standalone imports are different from persisted-draft loading. Imported `formData` represents prefilled current values and must remain in `formData`; do not run the all-field draft migration on the imported subtree. A legacy field-specific normalizer may remain as a compatibility wrapper, but it should delegate to the shared utility rather than duplicate its algorithm.
+- On save, strip default-backed field IDs from root and subtemplate `formData` so configured defaults are not persisted twice. Preserve the sanitized state returned to the builder and avoid mutating the caller’s form-data object.
+- Configuration editors must expose the default-value control immediately below Placeholder when both settings exist. Default editing must remain available while Disabled is selected so authors do not need to toggle the field just to set its initial value.
+
+### Runtime clearing must not reapply defaults
+
+Treat a configured default as an initialization fallback, not as a value that continuously competes with the user's runtime edits. A user must be able to clear a default-backed field and save the empty result.
+
+- Distinguish a missing value from an explicit runtime clear. Depending on the field contract, an explicit clear may be `''`, `null`, `false`, or `[]` for a multi-select. An absent or `undefined` value is not a portable persisted clear: follow the field’s serialized empty representation. Do not run normalization that converts an intentional empty value into `undefined` and then immediately falls back to the configured default.
+- Save-time reads must merge raw Angular control values over any hydrated/session snapshot values, without calling default resolution again. Raw values such as `''`, `null`, and `[]` must overwrite the old/default-backed value in the save payload.
+- Runtime controls must bind to the actual Angular form control when the form is interactive. Do not bind a computed `defaultValue`-backed expression with `[ngModel]` in a way that can write a stale default back during change detection before the form snapshot updates. Builder previews may use default resolution; runtime controls need current-control precedence.
+- Conditional rules can destroy and recreate runtime controls. When the processed container changes, hydrate newly registered controls once from the retained runtime snapshot (`currentValues`) after the view mounts. Do not resolve configured defaults again during this remount step: the snapshot was default-hydrated once and already preserves explicit `''`, `null`, and `[]` clears. Do not restore remounted controls with a continuously computed `[ngModel]` expression, because that can replay a configured default before the snapshot catches up.
+- Composite controls need an explicit clear path for every subcontrol. Verify date-only clearing, time-only clearing, single-select clearing, and multi-select clear-all independently. If a shared value accessor fails to propagate an empty subcontrol value, fix the application boundary or the shared source as appropriate, and test the currently consumed artifact.
+- The bug commonly appears after a short audit/debounce window: the control emits an empty value immediately, while a delayed session snapshot still contains the configured default. Test the sequence of setting a default, clearing it at runtime, saving immediately, and hydrating the saved empty value again.
+- The same stale-value bug can appear when a rule hides a field and later shows it again. Test both a populated value and an explicit clear across hide → show, and assert that the recreated control receives the retained raw value rather than the configured default.
+- Add regression coverage at the shared default utility, renderer save-read, runtime field adapter/template, and host-container boundaries as applicable. Assert that configured defaults still appear for missing values, while explicit clears remain empty after change detection, save, and reload.
+
+### Disabled configuration and rules
+
+Configurable form fields use the optional boolean `renderer.config.disabled` property:
+
+- missing and `false` mean enabled;
+- the field editor exposes the same Disabled checkbox for every supported field;
+- the checkbox writes the renderer configuration rather than a separate item-level flag;
+- runtime disabled state combines the field configuration with external/read-only state; and
+- rule-driven disable/enable effects preserve the rest of the renderer configuration.
+
+An enable rule may override a field’s configured `disabled: true` state, but it must not bypass an inherited disabled state from a parent container or external read-only mode. Use explicit boolean checks rather than truthiness checks when resolving the configured state.
+
+Do not materialize `disabled: false` into existing definitions or add migration code unless the field-specific design explicitly requires it. Boundary normalization for a newly defined contract must not silently rewrite persisted definitions.
