+# Validation and control identity
+
+Use the centralized validation configuration for fields that support validation:
+
+- Reuse `ValidationConfigEditorComponent` for validation type selection and custom error-message editing rather than duplicating validation UI in each field editor.
+- Persist the canonical form-definition property `validationType`; keep component-library-specific flags such as `fsValidationRequired` at the adapter/runtime boundary.
+- For standardized fields, keep the validation type and custom-message properties consistent in the form schema; do not persist a translated fallback message.
+- When compatibility is part of the field-specific design, read legacy field definitions at the adapter boundary and normalize them into the editor/runtime model without requiring an immediate data migration. Legacy compatibility is opt-in per field design; do not add an automatic migration.
+- Show the translated default message as the error-message placeholder. Persist a custom message only when the author enters one; resolve the default again at runtime so it remains translatable.
+- Clear stale custom messages when validation is removed.
+- Ensure the rendered component receives the library-specific validation configuration required for client-side enforcement.
+- Confirm required behavior on the actual runtime form, not only in the config modal.
+- When validation includes constraints, default errors should explain the applicable constraint or constraints. A custom error message remains an explicit author override.
+
+### Clearing submit-time validation
+
+The component-library form handler can own validation state in both the Angular control and the rendered error element. A submit-time `customError` may remain visible when a nested custom value accessor emits its native event before `NgModel` has committed the new value.
+
+- Refresh validation after the field value is committed, using the handler’s `refreshValidationMessages([controlName])` API so the control errors, error text, and validation classes are updated together.
+- Scope the refresh to the field that changed. Do not refresh every control on every form change; that can mark untouched fields as touched, display unrelated errors, and create change-detection loops.
+- Treat validation refresh as a repeatable operation. It must reuse the existing error element rather than append another one; add a regression check that repeated refreshes leave one visible error container per field.
+- Do not clear only `control.setErrors(null)` when the form handler owns the visual message. That can leave stale text or classes in the DOM; let the handler recompute the field.
+- For custom value accessors, keep `writeValue` one-way. If a shared control incorrectly echoes programmatic writes through `onChange`, identify the owning source, correct the value propagation contract, and verify the consumed library artifact; do not hide a deterministic defect with an adapter guard.
+- Composite controls can have multiple value-event paths, such as native input, autocomplete selection, explicit selection outputs, and clear actions. Define one canonical parent propagation path, deduplicate equivalent values, and refresh validation once the parent control contains the committed value.
+
+The component library does not necessarily render required markers consistently. If a control receives required validation but has no asterisk, add the marker at the application rendering layer. Keep the visual weight consistent with neighboring field labels.
+
+### Preserve Angular and form-handler control identity
+
+Angular `NgModel` can register a control through the component input `[name]`, while the component library’s `FsFormHandlerDirective` locates controls using a DOM query such as `[name="controlName"]`. Some `fs-*` components do not reflect their `name` input onto the host element.
+
+For a shared `fs-*` control whose host is the element recognized by the form handler, bind both:
+
+```html
+[name]="fieldName" [attr.name]="fieldName"
+```
+
+The host attribute lets the form handler find the component and inspect its inner control. Missing it can cause `formElement is null`, `tagName` errors, missing validation messages, and broken submit validation.
+
+For an application wrapper around a shared control, verify which element the form handler will find first. A wrapper can correctly receive `[name]` for Angular registration while still being the wrong DOM validation target. In that case, keep the Angular `[name]` input on the wrapper, put the DOM `name` on the inner control, and ensure the form handler’s query resolves to the actual input or recognized shared-control host. Do not add `[attr.name]` to a wrapper merely because the wrapped control needs a name.
+
+Test both identities explicitly: assert that Angular registers the expected control and that a form-level `[name="controlName"]` lookup resolves to the element used for validation. Repeat the check after conditional rendering and configuration changes.
+
+When a control is conditionally hidden or rendered inside a lazily mounted view, it may remain registered with Angular while its DOM element is temporarily absent. Treat DOM lookups as nullable in both application code and shared directives. Skip DOM-only listener, error-message, and class operations when the element is unavailable, while preserving form-model operations such as reset or value updates. If validation listeners must work after the control is mounted later, make setup idempotent and retryable.
+
+When a shared directive assumes that every registered control has a live DOM element, fix that assumption in the component-library source rather than patching `node_modules`. Fix application control identity separately when the host element is genuinely missing its required name or registration attribute.
